== Names That Were Removed <pg-migrate-removed>

#idx("removed names")
Of the names that left the public headers, nearly all had no code behind
them or were internal to the library. The rows of #cmd("removed.tsv") say
which, in the column #cmd("now"):

#deflist(width: 1.6in,
  [#var("header") #var("name")], [use #var("name") from #var("header")
    instead.],
  [#cmd("crypto370") #var("header") #var("name")], [the name moved to
    crypto370.],
  [#cmd("internal")], [still in the library, but private. A program that
    uses it needs a decision, not a rewrite.],
  [#cmd("-")], [gone, with nothing in its place: declared but never
    implemented.],
)

The replacements a program is most likely to need:

#tab(caption: [Removed names and their replacements])[
  #table(columns: (1.4in, 1.6in, 1fr), align: left,
    [*1.x*], [*2.0*], [*Note*],
    [#cmd("bcopy()")], [#cmd("memmove()"), #cmd("<string.h>")], [*The first
      two arguments change places:* #cmd("bcopy(src, dst, n)") is
      #cmd("memmove(dst, src, n)").],
    [#cmd("stricmp()"), #cmd("strncmpi()")], [#cmd("strcasecmp()"),
      #cmd("strncasecmp()"), #cmd("<strings.h>")], [the same code under the
      POSIX names],
    [#cmd("__getcrt()"), #cmd("__getgrt()"), #cmd("__getwsa()")],
      [#cmd("__crtget()"), #cmd("__grtget()"), #cmd("__wsaget()")], [and
      likewise #cmd("__setcrt") to #cmd("__crtset"), #cmd("__rescrt") to
      #cmd("__crtres"), and so on],
    [#cmd("sha256_...()"), #cmd("blowfish_...()"), #cmd("base64_...()")],
      [crypto370], [same C names],
  )
] <pg-migrate-removed-tab>

== Interfaces That Changed Meaning <pg-migrate-meaning>

#idx("migration", "changed interfaces")
The maps place names. They cannot tell that a name kept its place and
changed what it means. These did -- find their uses and decide each one,
rather than rewriting them blindly.

#tab(caption: [Interfaces whose meaning changed in 2.0])[
  #table(columns: (1.1in, 1fr, 1fr), align: left,
    [*Name*], [*1.x*], [*2.0*],
    [#cmd("in_addr_t")], [#cmd("struct in_addr")], [#cmd("unsigned long"),
      the address itself. A cast to #cmd("in_addr_t *") for
      #cmd("inet_aton()") becomes #cmd("struct in_addr *").],
    [#cmd("inet_aton()")], [0 for an address, -1 for none\; second argument
      #cmd("in_addr_t *")], [1 for an address, 0 for none, as on BSD\;
      second argument #cmd("struct in_addr *")],
    [#cmd("__listpd()")], [when storage ran out, returned the members
      collected so far], [returns #cmd("NULL") with #cmd("errno")
      #cmd("ENOMEM"). A caller that reads #cmd("NULL") as "no members"
      reports an empty library. Better: move to #cmd("__walkpd()").],
    [#cmd("JESJOB"), #cmd("DSLIST")], [80 and 98 bytes], [96 and 104 bytes:
      new fields appended, every 1.x offset unchanged. The library allocates
      both, so a program only needs to be compiled again.],
  )
] <pg-migrate-meaning-tab>

Two more things met in the first programs migrated:

- *A private copy of a name that the library now has collides with it.*
  A program that brought its own #cmd("socklen_t"), #cmd("inet_addr()") or
  #cmd("inet_ntoa()") drops them and uses the library's. Note that
  #cmd("inet_ntoa()") returns #cmd("NULL") when the program has no run-time
  anchor, and shares one buffer between all tasks\; #cmd("inet_ntop()")
  into a buffer of your own has neither problem.
- *A library you publish* whose public header includes a LIBC/370 header
  cannot be used by a program that is still on 1.x once it is built against
  2.0. Publish its 2.0 build as a prerelease until its users have moved:
  MBT offers a prerelease only to a version range that names one.

== New in 2.0 <pg-migrate-new>

Nothing here has to change for a program to build against 2.0. Each entry
is something a program may have worked around, and may now drop.

#tab(caption: [New interfaces in 2.0])[
  #table(columns: (1.9in, 1fr), align: left,
    [*New*], [*Replaces*],
    [#cmd("__walkpd()") in #cmd("<mvs/dslist.h>"): a PDS directory member
      by member through a callback, nothing allocated], [#cmd("__listpd()"),
      one allocation per member, which a large library can exhaust the region
      with],
    [#cmd("idcams_sysprint()") in #cmd("<mvs/idcams.h>"): every SYSPRINT
      line of IDCAMS with its message number], [reading the condition code
      of #cmd("idcams()"), which cannot tell "not found" from "refused"],
    [#cmd("inet_addr()"), #cmd("inet_pton()"), #cmd("inet_ntop()"),
      #cmd("inet_ntoa()") in #cmd("<arpa/inet.h>")], [#cmd("sscanf()") of
      the four parts, private copies],
    [#cmd("JESJOB.submit_time64"), #cmd("JESJOB.sysid") in
      #cmd("<mvs/jes2.h>")], [--],
    [#cmd("DSLIST.catnm") in #cmd("<mvs/dslist.h>"): the catalog in which
      #cmd("__listds()") found each entry], [--],
    [#cmd("fopen(")#var("dsn")#cmd(", \"w,unit=...,volser=...\")"): a new
      data set on the unit and volume named], [#cmd("__dsalcf()") with
      #cmd("UNIT=") and #cmd("VOLSER="), then #cmd("fopen()") of the DD],
  )
] <pg-migrate-new-tab>

== Verifying a Migrated Program <pg-migrate-verify>

- It builds against the 2.0 sysroot with #cmd("-Wall -Werror"). An implicit
  declaration means that step 3 of the procedure missed a name.
- Its generated assembler is the same as that of the 1.x build, source by
  source, with two exceptions: sources that differ only in the numbers of
  the compiler's labels (#cmd("@@F")#var("n"), #cmd("@@L")#var("n")),
  because a 1.x header carried a #cmd("static") function that the 2.0 one
  does not\; and sources that use one of the changed interfaces or a
  replaced name. Compile both sides with #cmd("-S") and compare. Any other
  difference is a missed include.

== Changes After 2.0.0 <pg-migrate-since>

What later 2.x releases ask of a program or a build:

#tab(caption: [Changes since 2.0.0])[
  #table(columns: (0.8in, 1fr, 1fr), align: left,
    [*Release*], [*Change*], [*What to do*],
    [2.1.0], [Needs CC/370 1.1.0 or later. Every header stops with
      #cmd("#error") otherwise.], [Install a current cc370.],
    [2.1.0], [The compiler's helper routines, such as the 64-bit division,
      moved to CC/370's #cmd("libcc370rt.a").], [A build that runs ld370
      itself adds #cmd("-lcc370rt").],
    [2.2.0], [#cmd("setbuf()") returns #cmd("void").], [Stop using its
      return value.],
    [2.3.0], [#cmd("PDPMAIN"), #cmd("PDP370"), #cmd("PDP380"),
      #cmd("PDP390") and #cmd("PDPORIG") are no longer shipped.],
      [Compile again with cc370 the C source of any assembler that copies
      #cmd("PDPMAIN"). Remove the old members from the sysroot's
      #cmd("macros") directory by hand: #cmd("make install") does not
      delete.],
    [2.4.0], [Needs CC/370 1.4.0 or later. Every header stops with
      #cmd("#error") otherwise.], [Install CC/370 1.4.0 or later. An MBT
      project needs MBT 2.2.0 or later.],
    [2.4.0], [#cmd("crt0.o") and #cmd("crt1.o") are no longer installed.
      The start-up routine #cmd("@@CRT0") is a member of #cmd("libc.a"),
      which a program's #cmd("main()") pulls in (@pg-startup-variants).
      #cmd("crtm.o") stays.], [Drop #cmd("crt0.o") or #cmd("crt1.o") from
      a link line that names it. With MBT, leave out the key
      #cmd("startup"), or keep #cmd("\"crtm\"").],
    [2.4.0], [#cmd("__premain()") is called before the standard streams are
      opened and before #cmd("main()").], [Replace a private
      #cmd("@@START") with it (@pg-startup-premain).],
    [2.4.1], [A text stream drops only the bytes X'00' at the end of a
      record. Up to 2.4.0 the line ended at the first X'00', and the rest
      of the record was lost.], [Nothing, unless a program relied on the
      cut: it now receives the bytes after an X'00' as well.],
    [2.5.0], [A stream takes its lock only after the first
      #cmd("cthread_create()").], [Up to 2.6.0: a program that attaches
      subtasks of its own and shares a stream with them serializes the
      stream itself. From 2.6.1 on: nothing.],
    [2.5.0], [#cmd("printf()"), #cmd("fprintf()") and #cmd("vfprintf()")
      return a negative value on an output error, and #cmd("fprintf()")
      writes results of 8192 characters and more in full.], [Test the
      return value for a negative value where the count is used.],
    [2.5.0], [#cmd("tmpfile()") opens #cmd("\"wb+\"").], [Nothing\; a
      program that opened a #cmd("tmpnam()") name to read its data back can
      use #cmd("tmpfile()").],
    [2.6.0], [#cmd("fopen(\"*GETLINE\", \"r\")") reads through the TSO
      GETLINE.], [Nothing\; a file name #cmd("*GETLINE") no longer opens a
      DD called #cmd("GETLINE").],
    [2.6.1], [A stream skips its lock only when the task has no subtask and
      no task above it runs C code. A C module with a start-up of its own,
      linked to on a thread, takes the lock again, and so, from the library
      source and not verified on MVS, does a subtask the program attaches
      itself.], [Nothing.],
    [2.6.2], [#cmd("fseek()") to a position inside the current record of a
      stream opened #cmd("\"r\"") lands there, and #cmd("ftell()")
      reports it. Up to 2.6.1 the stream was placed wrongly within the
      record or in front of it, and #cmd("ftell()") kept the old
      position.], [Nothing.],
    [2.6.3], [#cmd("rand()") returns values from 0 to #cmd("RAND_MAX"), and
      without #cmd("srand()") its sequence is the one #cmd("srand(1)")
      starts. Up to 2.6.2 it returned only 0 to 4095 and 32768 to 36863,
      and the first call without #cmd("srand()") returned 0.], [Nothing,
      unless a program depends on the exact numbers: every sequence
      differs from 2.6.2's.],
    [2.7.0], [The packages and the Homebrew formula depend on maclib370
      1.0.0 or later, below 2: SYS1.MACLIB and SYS1.AMODGEN of MVS 3.8j,
      installed into #cmd("<sysroot>/maclib370/macros"). The library
      itself is unchanged.], [#cmd("apt") or #cmd("dnf"): name maclib370's
      package beside LIBC/370's. Homebrew: #cmd("brew trust mvslovers/tap")
      first. Tarball: unpack maclib370's tarball into
      #cmd("<sysroot>/maclib370").],
    [2.8.0], [Needs CC/370 1.6.0 or later. The MVS system macros are no
      longer installed in #cmd("<sysroot>/macros")\; they come from
      maclib370, #cmd("<sysroot>/maclib370/macros"), which the assembler of
      CC/370 1.6.0 searches by itself. #cmd("fclose()") is 8 bytes longer
      (#cmd("FREEPOOL") at the TK5 maintenance level).], [Install CC/370
      1.6.0 or later. A build that names #cmd("<sysroot>/macros") with
      #cmd("-I") or #cmd("AS370_MACLIB") adds
      #cmd("<sysroot>/maclib370/macros").],
    [2.8.1], [The sysroot carries #cmd("share/libc370/sysmac-removed.txt"),
      the names of the MVS system macros earlier releases installed in
      #cmd("<sysroot>/macros").], [After an upgrade that unpacked the
      tarball, delete those names from #cmd("<sysroot>/macros"): a copy
      left there comes before maclib370's member.],
    [2.9.0], [COBOL and other code that calls with the OS linkage can call a
      C function through #cmd("@@CCALL") and a stub made by the macro
      #cmd("CCALL") (@pg-asm-ccall).], [Nothing. Code that called C
      directly from COBOL failed and can use the stub.],
    [2.10.0], [A COBOL main program, or any program that is not C, can
      call C functions through #cmd("@@CCALL"): the first call builds the
      C environment, #cmd("CCTERM") releases it
      (@pg-asm-ccall-main). U3101 now means a C thread or a subtask of a
      C program.], [Call #cmd("CCTERM") before #cmd("STOP RUN"), or
      buffered C output and #cmd("atexit()") functions are lost.],
    [2.10.1], [On a KSDS opened with #cmd("VSACCESS_DYNAM"),
      #cmd("vswrite()") writes direct: a lower key is inserted, and a
      duplicate fails with reason code 8 instead of 12.
      #cmd("vsread()") of a key that is not there reports reason code 16
      instead of 88.], [Nothing. A direct-#cmd("PUT") workaround can go.],
    [2.10.2], [#cmd("__listds()") returns a data set or catalog name that
      contains #cmd("-") whole, and its filter sees the whole name. Up to
      2.10.1 the name ended at the first #cmd("-").], [Nothing.],
    [2.10.3], [A member whose write failed -- out of space, or an I/O
      error -- is not put into the directory when the stream is closed: a
      new member does not appear, an existing one keeps its old content.
      Up to 2.10.2 a failed new member appeared with no records and a
      failed overwrite emptied the old one.], [Nothing.],
    [2.11.0], [#cmd("tryinfo()") returns the PSW and register 14 of the
      abend the last #cmd("try()") caught (@pg-errors-try). The CRT words
      #cmd("crtavail") at +17C are now #cmd("crttrpsw") and
      #cmd("crttrr14").], [Nothing. Code that used #cmd("crtavail")
      renames it.],
    [2.12.0], [A data set that runs out of space at its 16th extent, or on
